<Project>

  <!--
    Puts the client/server executables beside a test assembly that never references them.

    ClientServerUtilities' static constructor calls IsAuthenticodeTrusted() on each of them, which throws
    when the file is missing, and that is deliberate: the type is a setup component of the PowerShell
    module and a missing client is meant to fail loudly. It does mean the type cannot initialize unless
    those executables sit where it looks for them, and anything reading it fails with a
    TypeInitializationException instead.

    For a project hosting the PowerShell fixture the reader is TokenManager, reached while an
    EnvironmentTable enumerates logged-on sessions. Its decision short-circuits before asking about the
    client/server directory unless the caller is an administrator, so an unelevated run never initializes
    the type and never notices the executables are absent. An elevated one does, and every test that
    builds an EnvironmentTable then fails. Importing this is what makes both runs the same.

    The lookup resolves to the net472 directory beside PSADT.dll, so both target frameworks of an
    importing project land on the same folder: net472 is its own output directory, and net8.0 walks up
    one level and back down into net472. Building the client project and copying its four executables
    there once therefore satisfies both runs.

    Ordering is expressed as a ProjectReference rather than by invoking the Build target imperatively. An
    MSBuild task asking for Build races the solution's own build of the same project and the two collide
    writing its output; a ProjectReference goes through MSBuild's project instance cache and builds once.
    GetClientVariantOutputs below resolves to that same instance, so it reports the four executables
    without adding a second builder.

    UndefineProperties drops TargetFramework from what the reference passes down, and it has to. That
    project is single-targeted, so a solution build builds it with no TargetFramework global property at
    all. Handing it one, which is what SetTargetFramework does, produces a second, differently-keyed
    project instance that the instance cache cannot match against the first, and the two then build
    concurrently into the same output. Undefining it makes both requests the same instance.

    The reference is declared for net472 alone. Both target frameworks resolve to the same destination,
    so one is enough, and it keeps the net8.0 inner build from asking a net472-only project for a target
    it does not have. ReferenceOutputAssembly is false because nothing importing this compiles against it.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ClientServerProject Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\src\PSADT.ClientServer.Client\PSADT.ClientServer.Client.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net472'">
    <ProjectReference Include="@(ClientServerProject)" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" UndefineProperties="TargetFramework" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <Target Name="CopyClientServerExecutables" AfterTargets="Build" Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net472'">
    <MSBuild Projects="@(ClientServerProject)" Targets="GetClientVariantOutputs" Properties="Configuration=$(Configuration)" RemoveProperties="TargetFramework" BuildInParallel="false">
      <Output TaskParameter="TargetOutputs" ItemName="_ClientServerTargetPath" />
    </MSBuild>
    <Copy SourceFiles="@(_ClientServerTargetPath)" DestinationFolder="$(OutputPath)" SkipUnchangedFiles="true" />
  </Target>

</Project>
